      "heading": "Lead",
      "excerpt": "Aratus of Sicyon (Ancient Greek: Ἄρατος ὁ Σικυώνιος; 271–213 BC) was a politician and military commander of Hellenistic Greece. He was elect",
      "revisionUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1379837199"
    },
    {
      "id": "P2",
      "hash": "8acabc2d75910c9b4f6587ecabaf65c22f98353d6ea3ff92215f03e5995763df",
      "heading": "Lead",
      "excerpt": "Aratus was exiled to Argos at the age of seven, after his father, the magistrate of Sicyon, was killed in a coup. In 251 BC, he led an exped",
      "revisionUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1379837199"
    },
    {
      "id": "P3",
      "hash": "8caa8544956732e8350d0ce472be4bbdcdde2b7b9de65b357c86d4e2f6da6ec8",
      "heading": "Lead",
      "excerpt": "After conquering the Acrocorinth, Aratus pursued the Achaean League's expansion. When the Spartan king Cleomenes III conquered the Achaean c",
      "revisionUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1379837199"
    },
    {
      "id": "P4",
      "hash": "ca2dfa55c4e0580adc7d6c147960ed053d0989f4832809f72f4c8349c726fea5",
      "heading": "Youth",
      "excerpt": "Aratus was born in 271 BC in the northern Peloponnese city-state of Sicyon. His father, Cleinias of Sicyon, had become head magistrate of th",
      "revisionUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1379837199"
    },
    {
      "id": "P5",
      "hash": "b972bcf060f70d7b752024539f69efedc96b835c080d64afe87eabaa7d379f06",
      "heading": "Cleomenes gains the upper hand",
      "excerpt": "In 226 BC, Cleomenes conquered Mantinea, Tegea and Pharae. He succeeded in luring the Achaeans into open battle in the fall of 226 BC. Detai",
      "revisionUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1379837199"
    },
    {
      "id": "P6",
      "hash": "86dc897abda540c5108cd5a54cb031ab9918ad9d468abd497598d89944baa754",
      "heading": "Youth",
      "excerpt": "In Argos, Aratus initially became known as a good athlete, winning a prize in the pentathlon. But he remained focused on the idea of freeing",
      "revisionUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1379837199"
    },
    {
      "id": "P7",
      "hash": "2268884c1f4f58aaaa9a581dec39e036c1d0610820169cc07aa06774523812a9",
      "heading": "Liberating Sicyon",
      "excerpt": "In 251 BC, Aratus had originally intended to take and hold a fortified post near Sicyon from which he could raid his enemies' property, and ",
      "revisionUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1379837199"
    },
    {
      "id": "P8",
      "hash": "d7fd2b24aa09e0da3fe90d1054ff0240d23401c38d24973b50e1a7b552c60bd8",
      "heading": "Consolidating Sicyon",
      "excerpt": "Aratus' first act after taking Sicyon was to call back its exiles. According to Plutarch, he recalled 80 who had been banished by Nicocles, ",
      "revisionUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1379837199"
    },
    {
      "id": "P9",
      "hash": "4634b0bcdc8399bdf7c06ac3e61a1afc255702716e0dd67cbf2c4cea4b5ee9bc",
      "heading": "Taking the Acrocorinth",
      "excerpt": "In 245 BC, Aratus was elected strategos of the Achaean League. He went on to hold this position every two years until his death, with only a",
      "revisionUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1379837199"
    },
    {
      "id": "P10",
      "hash": "2ed67ff6f68b04908cf3f5bd4f9abdbeb95ae640822e1d1c86b00536522b338a",
      "heading": "Alliance with Sparta",
      "excerpt": "After its capture by Aratus, Corinth also joined the Achaean League. It was followed shortly afterwards by Megara, Troezen, and Epidaurus. T",
      "revisionUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1379837199"
    },
    {
      "id": "P11",
      "hash": "331b150fdd6630ae4a3ec8e5edc0098171a655bff1af6c2093a3f129cdca751c",
      "heading": "Setbacks at Argos",
      "excerpt": "After the death of Antigonus, the Achaean and Aetolian Leagues allied with each other. The reason behind this alliance remains obscure, and ",
      "revisionUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1379837199"
    },
    {
      "id": "P12",
      "hash": "154327f5e9b44f4d22d70b10370edb50487f20fb5fdc564a03e9d812b75ddff6",
      "heading": "Annexation of Megalopolis",
      "excerpt": "The city of Megalopolis was founded in 368 BC by the members of the Arcadian League as a stronghold against Sparta. Until 235 BC, its tyrant",
      "revisionUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1379837199"
    },
    {
      "id": "P13",
      "hash": "54ac821996ce4418dc32891ff42c7725a17c2216e72dde38c5a13b6a4159ef1a",
      "heading": "War against Demetrius",
      "excerpt": "Realising that the Achaean League had become a major player in Greece with the incorporation of Megalopolis, Demetrius II, the new king of M",
      "revisionUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1379837199"
    },
    {
      "id": "P14",
      "hash": "cc9b934eb67b6e1010a4134ee3ad3c55cc02184ee7ef5402dbfcc4204508729b",
      "heading": "Cleomenes gains the upper hand",
      "excerpt": "In 229 BC, Cleomenes III of Sparta took three Arcadian cities from the Aetolian League: Tegea, Orchomenus, and Mantinea. It is unclear why t",
      "revisionUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1379837199"
    },
    {
      "id": "P15",
      "hash": "1ecfcca7c25dee6f4040824f099615de79794ec2d2d9144deff8ddda88cb4c8f",
      "heading": "Macedonian intervention",
      "excerpt": "Shortly before the start of the siege, the Achaean council members decided to accept Macedon's war conditions after all. Aratus sent his son",
